With iOS 27.2 and tvOS 27.2, second- and third-generation <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> Remotes can be tracked using the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/find-my/">Find My</a> app.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/ios-27-2-apple-tv-remote.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1073507" /><br/>
There has been an option to locate a lost &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remote with an iPhone since iOS 17, but remotes did not previously show up in the &zwnj;Find My&zwnj; app. Locating a remote had to be done with the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a> Remote widget in the Control Center, which made the option hard to find.<br/>
<br/>
Supported &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remotes are now listed in the &zwnj;Find My&zwnj; app right alongside other devices, a more intuitive option for iOS and macOS users.<br/>
<br/>
With the upcoming fourth-generation &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; 4K, Apple is adding a speaker and Precision Finding to the &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remote to make it easier to find when misplaced. Older remotes are not able to play a sound and do not have Precision Finding.<br/>
<br/>
When the fourth-generation &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; was announced, Apple said &zwnj;Find My&zwnj; support would be available on the 2nd-generation &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remote and later, and it's here now. <br/>
<br/>
